\f0\fs24 \cf0 A simple, informative model to begin with would be:\
\
1) Just a few minutes after the Big Bang, the baryon density is similar to that of the atmosphere on the surface of Mars. \
2) The photon density is so extreme that the photons that became free to travel at recombination still comprise 99.9% of the photons\'97 and over 90% of the photon energy\'97 in the universe today\
3) The strong force has essentially done what it's going to do for the next 100 million years and can be ignored\
4) The electrostatic force is so strong that it keeps the universe electrically neutral to a very good approximation\
5) Gravity has no "negative charge," so, unlike electric potential, gravitational potential accumulates in any region with a higher-than-average density. \
6) Locally, photon energy and pressure increases almost instantaneously as gravity compresses the plasma, resulting in an extraordinary speed of sound in the medium. \
7) Photons push around electrons, which pull on the more massive baryons, which pull back on the electrons, which push back on the photons, etc. We can ignore the relatively small electron mass in our initial model, but the relationship between electrons and photons will be extremely important in the recombination era. \
\
I think that explanation is enough to introduce a simulator where a user can manipulate baryon and photon densities and see some resulting behavior, perhaps something that can track forward to simulate the resulting CMB. Energies are relativistic, but we can build a model of particles that are moving slowly enough to observe, and keep in mind that essentially everything is moving locally at some significant fraction of the speed of light. \
\
What do you think? \
\
\
Although we imagine the universe a few minutes after the Big Bang as incredibly dense, you might be surprised to learn that a neutron star is roughly 20 orders of magnitude denser, similar to the ratio of the length of a football field to the diameter of the Milky Way galaxy. The universe was filled with a plasma of light atomic nuclei, electrons, and photons, but the characteristic distance between massive particles was on the scale of nanometers (10^\{-9\}), not the femtometers (10^\{-15\} required for fusion. The strong nuclear force would not have much of an influence for another hundred million years. \
\
The universe was also almost entirely electrically neutral, because, counterintuitively, electrical forces are so strong. The ratio of the strength of gravity to the strength of the electric force between particles is comparable to the difference between the size of an atom and the size of our observable universe. Any charge separation produces an enormous restoring electric field, so that positive and negative electric charges track each other meticulously. \
\
However, gravity has no \'91negative charge.\'92 Mass, and therefore potential, accumulated over large regions, and potential wells began to pull massive particles together. \
\
That gives you a wonderful transition from the Coulomb widget:\
\
Coulomb forces dominate individual charged-particle interactions so strongly that they rapidly suppress macroscopic charge separation. Ironically, that is why we can largely remove electrostatics from the large-scale acoustic problem.\
